1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,643
Put a number on each end of this line.

2
00:00:02,643 --> 00:00:06,191
Replace each number by the number at its neighbour.

3
00:00:06,191 --> 00:00:12,104
Two numbers simply swap places. This tiny operation will lead to a question about how

4
00:00:12,104 --> 00:00:13,704
large a network can be.

5
00:00:14,100 --> 00:00:17,343
A graph is a collection of dots joined by lines.

6
00:00:17,343 --> 00:00:22,477
Its adjacency table has a one when two dots are joined and a zero otherwise.

7
00:00:22,477 --> 00:00:27,612
Multiplying by this table means adding the numbers at each dot's neighbours.

8
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:32,400
Start with one at each end. After the swap, nothing changes.

9
00:00:32,400 --> 00:00:35,920
The pattern has been multiplied by positive one.

10
00:00:35,920 --> 00:00:42,520
A pattern that only gets scaled is an eigenvector, and the scale factor is its eigenvalue.

11
00:00:42,900 --> 00:00:47,151
Now start with one and minus one. The swap reverses the signs.

12
00:00:47,151 --> 00:00:50,649
The new pattern is minus one times the old pattern.

13
00:00:50,649 --> 00:00:54,900
This gives an eigenvalue of negative one: the direction flips.

14
00:00:55,300 --> 00:00:59,135
A graph with n dots has n eigenvalues, counting repeats.

15
00:00:59,135 --> 00:01:03,519
Because its adjacency table is symmetric, they are real numbers.

16
00:01:03,519 --> 00:01:06,943
We count how many are positive, zero and negative.

17
00:01:06,943 --> 00:01:09,820
These three counts are called the inertia.

18
00:01:10,200 --> 00:01:14,092
A triangle has eigenvalues two, minus one, minus one.

19
00:01:14,092 --> 00:01:19,820
One positive and two negative. A pentagon has three positive and two negative.

20
00:01:19,820 --> 00:01:24,960
So the same negative count can coexist with different positive counts.

21
00:01:25,333 --> 00:01:29,748
We fix the negative count at four. How many positive values are possible?

22
00:01:29,748 --> 00:01:34,950
And how many dots can a network have? Those are different questions, because some dots

23
00:01:34,950 --> 00:01:38,941
can add zero eigenvalues without adding positive or negative ones.

24
00:01:39,333 --> 00:01:44,759
Copy one endpoint of our original edge, giving the copy exactly the same neighbours.

25
00:01:44,759 --> 00:01:49,345
Put one on the original, minus one on the copy, and zero in the middle.

26
00:01:49,345 --> 00:01:53,349
Every neighbour sum is zero. The copied difference disappears.

27
00:01:53,733 --> 00:02:00,142
Copies leave the positive and negative counts unchanged, though the individual eigenvalues

28
00:02:00,142 --> 00:02:03,276
may change. Lonely dots also add only zeros.

29
00:02:03,276 --> 00:02:08,901
To ask a finite size question, remove these easy ways of making a graph bigger.

30
00:02:09,300 --> 00:02:14,667
A reduced graph has no lonely dots and no two dots with identical sets of neighbours.

31
00:02:14,667 --> 00:02:17,319
Reduced does not mean no zero eigenvalues.

32
00:02:17,319 --> 00:02:22,812
Other linear dependencies can still create zeros, and they matter for our size problem.

33
00:02:23,200 --> 00:02:27,574
The answer for four negatives is thirty dots in a reduced graph.

34
00:02:27,574 --> 00:02:30,375
Exactly four reduced graphs reach thirty.

35
00:02:30,375 --> 00:02:35,227
Each has four positive, twenty-two zero, and four negative eigenvalues.

36
00:02:35,227 --> 00:02:40,216
The zeros explain why thirty dots need not mean thirty active directions.

37
00:02:40,600 --> 00:02:44,471
The largest possible positive count is eleven, achieved by two reduced graphs with fifteen

38
00:02:44,471 --> 00:02:48,342
dots and no zeros. The numerical bound and these examples were already supplied by earlier

39
00:02:48,342 --> 00:02:51,568
work. The classification proves that no other reduced graph attains eleven.

40
00:02:51,967 --> 00:02:55,049
How can a finite computation settle all graphs?

41
00:02:55,049 --> 00:03:01,213
First keep an induced core with the same positive and negative counts but no zero eigenvalues.

42
00:03:01,213 --> 00:03:06,919
Induced means that, among the dots you keep, you retain every original connecting line.

43
00:03:07,300 --> 00:03:10,265
The number of dots in a core is its rank.

44
00:03:10,265 --> 00:03:17,063
With no zero eigenvalues, its table is nonsingular: an inverse table can undo the calculation.

45
00:03:17,063 --> 00:03:23,500
Reduced graphs can be larger than their cores, so we must add zero directions back later.

46
00:03:23,867 --> 00:03:29,978
The second ingredient says every nonsingular graph can be built from a smaller one by

47
00:03:29,978 --> 00:03:36,088
adding one dot, or sometimes two. Two are essential: our first edge has a nonsingular

48
00:03:36,088 --> 00:03:39,971
two-by-two table, while a lone dot has the zero table.

49
00:03:40,367 --> 00:03:45,813
Why does the rule cover every case? Jacobi's determinant identity examines the inverse

50
00:03:45,813 --> 00:03:51,260
table. Either a nonzero diagonal entry permits one deletion, or a nonzero off-diagonal

51
00:03:51,260 --> 00:03:56,327
entry permits two. The paper proves these alternatives leave a nonsingular core.

52
00:03:56,700 --> 00:04:01,819
For each core, try every possible zero-or-one list of connections to a new dot.

53
00:04:01,819 --> 00:04:05,578
A calculation using the inverse tells which sign is added.

54
00:04:05,578 --> 00:04:10,956
Written with the determinant and adjugate, its sign is decided using whole numbers.

55
00:04:11,333 --> 00:04:16,637
Watch a concrete case. Join the new dot to both ends of the edge, making a triangle.

56
00:04:16,637 --> 00:04:22,509
The connection list is one, one. The inverse table is the same swap table, so the calculation

57
00:04:22,509 --> 00:04:26,549
gives two. Its negative contributes one new negative eigenvalue.

58
00:04:26,933 --> 00:04:32,989
The program grows every allowed graph, merges copies made by renaming, and discards anything

59
00:04:32,989 --> 00:04:37,729
exceeding four negatives. It also tries the required two-dot extensions.

60
00:04:37,729 --> 00:04:42,797
This is exhaustive generation guided by a lemma, rather than a random search.

61
00:04:43,167 --> 00:04:47,944
For exactly four negatives, the number of cores rises and then falls.

62
00:04:47,944 --> 00:04:53,759
At fifteen dots, just two remain. At sixteen and seventeen, none remain even when we

63
00:04:53,759 --> 00:04:59,367
allow fewer than four negatives. These empty levels are the stopping certificate.

64
00:04:59,767 --> 00:05:04,308
Why are two empty levels enough? Suppose there were a first larger core.

65
00:05:04,308 --> 00:05:06,767
Remove one or two dots using the lemma.

66
00:05:06,767 --> 00:05:09,857
Its parent would have to sit on an earlier level.

67
00:05:09,857 --> 00:05:13,831
Starting from two empty levels, neither route can ever restart.

68
00:05:14,200 --> 00:05:19,704
The complete census contains forty-five thousand two hundred and forty-two cores with

69
00:05:19,704 --> 00:05:22,942
at most four negatives. Two implementations agree.

70
00:05:22,942 --> 00:05:28,899
Exact replay checks every recorded graph and its sign counts, and the mathematical extension

71
00:05:28,899 --> 00:05:31,360
rule is what establishes completeness.

72
00:05:31,733 --> 00:05:34,951
Now reconstruct a larger reduced graph from a core.

73
00:05:34,951 --> 00:05:38,484
Each extra dot gets a nonzero binary connection pattern.

74
00:05:38,484 --> 00:05:42,079
Its self-calculation must be zero to avoid changing rank.

75
00:05:42,079 --> 00:05:47,189
Between two added dots, the corresponding calculation must be either zero or one.

76
00:05:47,567 --> 00:05:52,735
Compatible patterns can coexist. An exact search finds the largest mutually compatible

77
00:05:52,735 --> 00:05:57,664
collection for every core. This is the method of Akbari, Cameron and Khosrovshahi.

78
00:05:57,664 --> 00:06:01,871
Applying it across all relevant ranks supplies the missing size bound.

79
00:06:02,267 --> 00:06:07,004
Here are the maximum reduced sizes for ranks five through fifteen.

80
00:06:07,004 --> 00:06:12,891
The peak is thirty, at rank eight. Above rank eight, the maxima fall below thirty.

81
00:06:12,891 --> 00:06:18,131
Excluding those higher ranks is the central new step in the size theorem.

82
00:06:18,500 --> 00:06:22,038
Thirty is actually attainable. Start with one edge.

83
00:06:22,038 --> 00:06:24,952
Copy every dot and its connection pattern.

84
00:06:24,952 --> 00:06:29,877
Join a new dot to all copies, then attach one more dot to that new dot.

85
00:06:29,877 --> 00:06:31,820
The old two dots become six.

86
00:06:32,200 --> 00:06:36,477
Repeat the same operation: six becomes fourteen, then thirty.

87
00:06:36,477 --> 00:06:41,946
A second version adds connections from the final new dot to the original dots.

88
00:06:41,946 --> 00:06:48,256
Using the variant first and the ordinary version afterwards gives the four maximum graphs.

89
00:06:48,633 --> 00:06:53,528
Those four rank-eight classes were already known to Akbari, Cameron and Khosrovshahi

90
00:06:53,528 --> 00:06:58,598
in two thousand and four. This work proves that allowing other ranks with four negative

91
00:06:58,598 --> 00:07:02,793
eigenvalues does not produce a bigger graph or another thirty-dot class.

92
00:07:03,167 --> 00:07:07,384
For the eleven-positive record, the first graph is called W five.

93
00:07:07,384 --> 00:07:10,368
Start with five mutually connected point dots.

94
00:07:10,368 --> 00:07:12,834
Add a dot for each of their ten pairs.

95
00:07:12,834 --> 00:07:18,479
Join each pair dot to its two points, and join pair dots when their pairs are disjoint.

96
00:07:18,867 --> 00:07:22,315
The other graph, X, begins with a three-by-three grid.

97
00:07:22,315 --> 00:07:27,807
Join points sharing a row or column. Add six dots for the wrap-around diagonals in the

98
00:07:27,807 --> 00:07:33,171
two directions. Join each to its three points, and join parallel line dots together.

99
00:07:33,567 --> 00:07:37,654
Each graph has fifteen eigenvalues: eleven positive and four negative.

100
00:07:37,654 --> 00:07:42,383
Their degree patterns differ, so they cannot be the same graph with renamed dots.

101
00:07:42,383 --> 00:07:47,638
The completed census and reconstruction checks show that these are the only reduced graphs

102
00:07:47,638 --> 00:07:49,215
attaining eleven positives.

103
00:07:49,600 --> 00:07:54,821
Charles, Farber, Johnson and Kennedy-Shaffer supplied both examples and the numerical

104
00:07:54,821 --> 00:07:57,769
bound underlying this result in twenty thirteen.

105
00:07:57,769 --> 00:08:02,929
The later inertia identity of Elphick and colleagues makes the implication explicit.

106
00:08:02,929 --> 00:08:07,720
The retained increment here is uniqueness, alongside independent verification.

107
00:08:08,100 --> 00:08:13,377
Keep the two records separate. Thirty is the maximum number of dots after copies and

108
00:08:13,377 --> 00:08:18,717
isolated dots are removed. Eleven is the maximum positive count, with four negatives.

109
00:08:18,717 --> 00:08:23,052
The size champions and positive-count champions are different graphs.

110
00:08:23,433 --> 00:08:28,548
The proof combines written linear-algebra arguments with complete exact computations.

111
00:08:28,548 --> 00:08:31,076
It is not a proof-assistant formalisation.

112
00:08:31,076 --> 00:08:36,973
Integer and rational tests make the decisions; decimal eigenvalue pictures are only illustrations.

113
00:08:36,973 --> 00:08:39,801
Both the lemmas and the computation are needed.

114
00:08:40,200 --> 00:08:45,192
For five negative eigenvalues, the corresponding maximum-size question remains open.

115
00:08:45,192 --> 00:08:48,282
A pattern in the small cases is a clue, not a proof.

116
00:08:48,282 --> 00:08:53,392
Failed searches through selected examples cannot replace the exhaustive generation and

117
00:08:53,392 --> 00:08:55,056
stopping argument used here.

118
00:08:55,433 --> 00:09:01,174
You have seen the whole route: moving numbers gives eigenvalues; removing zero directions

119
00:09:01,174 --> 00:09:06,206
gives cores; complete generation and compatible reconstruction give the bound.

120
00:09:06,206 --> 00:09:11,753
The paper and verification files contain the full arguments, exact census and explicit

121
00:09:11,753 --> 00:09:12,785
extremal graphs.
